cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its released checkpoints, please cite it."
title: "sourcetrace: open-set audio deepfake attribution with neural codec residuals (CoRA)"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-09-21
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/pujariaditya/CoRA"
abstract: >-
  Attribute a synthetic speech clip to the synthesis model that produced it, or
  reject it as a model never seen in training. A frozen WavLM-Large front-end, a
  52-d spectral signature and a 33-d neural-codec reconstruction residual feed a
  small gated head that produces one 288-d embedding; an anchor-margin plus
  relative-Mahalanobis score decides known against unseen, and the nearest anchor
  names the model. On the MLAAD v5 open-set protocol it reaches 0.50% FPR95
  against a published 3.36% with 99.86% known-model accuracy, and the same model
  applied to STOPA without retraining reaches 6.80% EER for held-out synthesis
  models.
authors:
  - family-names: Pujari
    given-names: Aditya
keywords:
  - audio-deepfake
  - source-tracing
  - open-set-recognition
  - neural-codec
  - encodec
  - wavlm
  - conformal-prediction
  - mlaad
  - stopa
